---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import CopyCommand from '../components/CopyCommand.astro'
import { GITHUB, LATEST, RELEASES, INSTALL_SH, PI_NPM } from '../lib/content'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Get Phosphor — installation and requirements"
  description="Install Phosphor for macOS, Linux, or Windows. Set up the pi coding agent, connect your model provider, and open your first local project."
>
  <header class="page-intro container">
    <p class="label muted">Phosphor / Get started</p>
    <h1 class="display">
      Your next project.<br /><span class="hero-title-end">Your own machine.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Phosphor is open source and MIT-licensed. Install the desktop app and the pi coding agent,
      then connect the model provider you want to work with.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="action" href={LATEST}>Open latest release <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a
        class="text-link"
        href="#setup">Follow the setup <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="section container" id="downloads">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">01 /</span><p class="label">The desktop app</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2 class="title">Pick your platform.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Download the matching asset from GitHub Releases. This site does not guess your
          architecture or silently choose an installer.
        </p>
        <ul class="platform-list">
          <li>
            <h3 class="subhead">macOS</h3><p>
              DMG or ZIP. Choose arm64 for Apple silicon, x64 for Intel. Drag Phosphor into
              Applications.
            </p><a class="text-link" href={LATEST}
              >Find the macOS download <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3 class="subhead">Linux</h3><p>
              AppImage or .deb, for x86_64 and arm64. Choose the format and architecture that match
              your system.
            </p><a class="text-link" href={LATEST}
              >Find the Linux download <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3 class="subhead">Windows</h3><p>
              NSIS .exe installer. Windows builds are produced by the tag-triggered Release
              workflow, not the per-merge workflow. Browse release assets for an .exe; it is not
              included in every release.
            </p><a class="text-link" href={RELEASES}
              >Browse Windows releases <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="setup" class="section container">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">02 /</span><p class="label">The first session</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <div class="install-step">
          <h2 class="subhead"><span>01</span>Install the engine.</h2>
          <p>
            Install <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download">Node.js 22.19 or newer</a>, then
            install <a href={PI_NPM}>pi</a> in your terminal. Phosphor needs the pi executable on your
            PATH.
          </p>
          <CopyCommand
            id="pi-command"
            label="Install pi · all platforms"
            command="npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent"
          />
        </div>
        <div class="install-step">
          <h2 class="subhead"><span>02</span>Install the window.</h2>
          <p>
            Use a download above. On macOS or Linux, you can use the installer below instead. It
            detects the platform and verifies the download against the release checksums.
          </p>
          <CopyCommand
            id="app-command"
            label="Install Phosphor · macOS and Linux"
            command={`curl -fsSL ${INSTALL_SH} | sh`}
          />
          <p>
            This command downloads and runs a shell script. <a
              href={`${GITHUB}/blob/main/scripts/install.sh`}>Read the installer source</a
            > first, or use a release asset directly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="install-step">
          <h2 class="subhead"><span>03</span>Bring a provider. Open a folder.</h2>
          <p>
            Launch Phosphor and connect a provider in Settings → Accounts. You can also run <code
              >pi</code
            > in the built-in terminal and use <code>/login</code>. API keys and local endpoints are
            configured through pi.
          </p>
          <p>
            Open a project folder, choose a model, and describe a small first task. Then inspect
            what changed.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/workbench#providers"
            >Check provider connection paths <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section work-surface">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">03 /</span><p class="label">Know the boundaries</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content prose">
        <h2 class="title">Local is not the same<br />as permission-gated.</h2>
        <p>
          pi runs with full tool permissions. The agent can edit files and execute commands without
          a Phosphor approval prompt. Start in a trusted project, use version control, and review
          the changes. Worktrees separate working directories; they are not a security sandbox.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your project and sessions live on your machine. Model requests and connected-tool requests
          go to the providers and services you configure. Provider fees and subscription limits
          still apply.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="section-index"><p class="label">A few practical notes</p></div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need another model subscription?</summary><p>
            Not necessarily. Use a supported subscription you already have, an API key, or a local
            endpoint. Claude Pro / Max requires the Claude Code provider package and official CLI.
            Phosphor does not include model usage.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What happens when an update is available?</summary><p>
            The packaged app checks for updates at launch and periodically afterward. The update
            path depends on your platform and package format; follow the app’s update action. <a
              href={`${GITHUB}/blob/main/docs/updates.md`}>Read the platform-specific details.</a>
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if Phosphor cannot find pi?</summary><p>
            Confirm Node.js meets the minimum version and that <code>pi --version</code> works in your
            terminal. Restart Phosphor after installation. The app’s setup screen reports missing pi;
            the desktop process may inherit a different PATH than your shell.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Where can I report a problem?</summary><p>
            <a href={`${GITHUB}/issues`}>Open a GitHub issue</a> with your platform, Phosphor and pi versions,
            and steps to reproduce it. Redact credentials and private project information from any logs
            you share.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
